frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Padouk Ab4 over a closed-open tube of Part II's measured A4 radius, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; everything else as the rendered Ab4 card.
name	padouk-ab4-tubetuned-rubber-x159-f4
source	Bar: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544: Bar No. 5 (G4, 397 Hz), xylophone CONCORDE X4001, padouk, L 29.3 cm, b 3.7 cm, h 1.9 cm edge; E, density, gamma_B through the packs named below. Eta 3.86e-7 s is read from Fig. 4 of the same paper (alpha = 6.11 + 7.62e-6 f^2, Eq. 19), 4.22 times Table I's 9.16e-8; why the two differ is not known and they may not be the same bar. Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in. Tube: V. Doutaut, D. Matignon and A. Chaigne, Numerical simulations of xylophones. II, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 104(3), 1633-1647 (1998): the radius of its A4 resonator, closed at one end, a_T = 1.85 cm (p.1641; measured in Fig. 9, p.1643). Gap: W. E. Flynt, The Construction and Tuning of Vibrating Bars, AMICA Bulletin, December 1972 and March 1973, revised for Mechanical Music Digest, January 2009: "About 1/4 to 1/2 inch spacing is common"; the 1/2 in end.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. No source prints a stop for this tube, so this card sets it as a maker would, at 193.921 mm, solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it so its first resonance sits on this bar's own computed f1 of 415.305 Hz, from this card's modes; no recording enters. NOT in the source either: the undercut's shape on this note (solved here in the engine on this card's 56 x 8 x 4 mesh, a circular arc, reach 146.471 mm, middle 10.821 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 as near 3 f1 as the arc allows, 3.003 in the render; the printed bar itself is tuned about 1:4 at 397 Hz, Table II; the 1:3 target is from xylophone tuning practice in print, not from the recording). The tube's radius is Part II's measured A4 resonator's, 18.5 mm, read as the inside radius and carried to this note, 1 semitone below A4; Part II names no instrument for that tube (its G4 comparisons are on the CONCORDE X4001 of Part I) and prints no wall or stopper. The gap is 12.7 mm below the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 8.2 mm from the mouth: the 1/2 in end of Flynt's printed 1/4 to 1/2 in, the end nearer Part II's "a few centimeters only", chosen before any render. The tube is a closed-open column of five odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 37.0 mm across inside, is as wide as this bar. Mallet: the printed rubber row, 1.0 m/s, strike 15.9 cm, Table I and Fig. 8. Iowa's bars are not known to be padouk.
